#
# busybox configuration for the Samsara port.
#
# This file is data, not a build product. ports/busybox/build.sh runs
# `make allnoconfig` to get a config with everything off, merges this file onto
# it with toolchain/merge-config, and then runs `oldconfig` so kconfig resolves
# the dependencies and reports any symbol in here that does not exist. Nothing
# here mentions a path, which is what keeps the file portable: the same config
# describes the same program on any machine.
#
# The selection rule was: a shell, and the programs needed to actually use one.
# The omissions are grouped by reason, because the reason is the useful part --
# each of these is a capability the kernel does not have yet, so the applet would
# not be a reduced version of itself but a program that fails when used.
#
#   Anything that reads /proc: ps, free, uptime, top, pmap, smemcap. There is no
#   procfs. These are not "ps without columns", they are programs whose only
#   source of data does not exist, and they would print an empty list rather
#   than admit they cannot answer.
#
#   Anything that reads a utmp file: who, users, last. Same shape of gap: the
#   kernel has no login accounting to record or to read.
#
#   Anything that needs statfs: df. The VFS has no per-filesystem statistics, so
#   there is nothing for it to report and a fabricated number would be worse
#   than no number. This is also why include/platform.h drops
#   HAVE_SYS_STATFS_H for this port.
#
#   Anything that opens a socket: the entire networking menu. There are no
#   sockets in the kernel, so these are not a reduced telnet but a program that
#   cannot be started.
#
#   Anything that opens a terminal the fancy way: vi, less, more, hexedit. vi and
#   less are ncurses clients, and ncurses is a library the size of the shell it
#   would serve. ed is here instead, which is small and does the job.
#
#   Anything that is a daemon or an init: there is no supervision to attach to
#   and nothing that wants to be pid 1. The kernel spawns the first program
#   directly and hands the framebuffer to a terminal.
#
#   setuid applets. CONFIG_INSTALL_APPLET_SYMLINKS is off and FEATURE_SUID is
#   off for the same reason the list is short: the port has one user, so a
#   privileged helper would be a way to run a program as root rather than a tool.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Link and install shape.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
CONFIG_STATIC=y
CONFIG_LFS=y

# `--long-options`, which `allnoconfig` leaves off and which nothing else in this
# file pulls in.
#
# It reads like a convenience option and is not one. With it off, every long
# option in busybox compiles out and `include/libbb.h` redirects `getopt32long` to
# plain `getopt32`, which knows only single-character options. So `ls --color`
# and `ls --full-time` are not merely unsupported: the option string they would
# have been parsed from is not in the binary, and every applet's long-option table
# is compiled away with it. What a user sees is a shell that rejects half the
# invocations they have used everywhere else.
#
# The applets affected are not a short list. `ls`, `cp`, `rm`, `grep`, `sed`,
# `find`, `sort`, `cut`, `readlink`, `realpath` and more all carry long options
# behind this one symbol, and `ls --color` additionally needs
# `CONFIG_FEATURE_LS_COLOR` to be meaningful -- which is already set below, and
# was doing nothing at all without this.
#
# Cost: a little more code in `libbb/getopt32.c`, which is where the long-option
# parser lives. No new dependency: it is busybox's own parser, and the reason it
# was off here is only that `allnoconfig` turns everything off and this file
# lists what the port wants rather than what a desktop wants.
CONFIG_LONG_OPTS=y

# How this port links, as a checked-in fact rather than a Makefile variable,
# because every part of it is an ABI fact and none of it is machine-specific.
#
# `CONFIG_STATIC` is not sufficient on its own and the distinction is the whole
# point of the flags below. `-static` alone produces an ET_EXEC linked at
# 0x400000; the kernel maps user images at their *link-time* addresses, load bias
# zero, exactly as Linux handles static-PIE. So an ET_EXEC busybox is loaded at an
# address nothing else in this system uses, while every other user image sits at
# 0x110000 -- and the ELF load fails with no message that names the cause.
#
#   -static-pie -fPIE   builds a position-independent image, so the flag is
#                        accepted by the driver and the link is relocation-safe
#   --image-base        puts it at 0x110000, the address every other user image
#                        in this system uses
#
# The result is still ET_EXEC rather than ET_DYN, because the kernel's loader maps
# a user image at its link-time virtual addresses with load bias zero -- exactly as
# Linux handles static-PIE -- and so the base address is not negotiable either way.
# What matters is that it agrees with the other images. An ET_EXEC busybox linked
# at 0x400000 by the default `-static` is loaded somewhere nothing else is, and the
# ELF load fails with no message that names the cause.
#
# These are the same flags user/build-chhello.sh uses, which is the point: there
# is one answer to "how does a C program get into this kernel" and every program
# is built with it.
#
# `--defsym=syscall=__do_syscall4` is mlibc's: this kernel's `syscall`
# instruction is not the one libc's generic syscall() emits, and every ported
# program -- chello, fbterm, busybox -- has to be told. The build script adds only
# the sysroot's -L, which is the one piece that genuinely differs per machine.
CONFIG_EXTRA_CFLAGS="-fPIE"
CONFIG_EXTRA_LDFLAGS="-nostdlib -static-pie -fPIE -Wl,--image-base=0x110000 -Wl,--defsym=syscall=__do_syscall4 -Wl,--build-id=none"

# Build one binary and let argv[0] choose the applet: /bin/ls, /bin/cat and the
# rest are all the same image, reached by name.
#
# Both settings below are about *reaching* that image. Upstream defaults
# BUSYBOX_EXEC_PATH to /proc/self/exe, and this port has no procfs, so an applet
# that shells out to another applet -- `find -exec`, `xargs`, the shell's own
# `exec` -- has no way to find itself and would fail. Naming the real path is the
# whole fix, and it is why the path appears here rather than being patched in.
#
# FEATURE_PREFER_APPLETS makes those same callers try the applet table before
# consulting PATH, so they keep working even where a link is missing. It is
# redundant once every applet has a link, and it is kept for the case where one
# does not: an applet found by table lookup is correct, while one that has to be
# found by PATH is one more thing that can go wrong in a system this small.
CONFIG_FEATURE_PREFER_APPLETS=y
CONFIG_BUSYBOX_EXEC_PATH="/bin/busybox"
CONFIG_INSTALL_APPLET_SYMLINKS=n

# `busybox --install -s /bin`.
#
# This is how /bin/ls comes to exist. busybox makes its own links rather than the
# kernel seeding them from a list, and the reason is that the list lives in
# busybox's generated applet table: a kernel-side list would be a second copy of
# it, stale the moment the port's config changes. A stale list means `/bin/foo`
# missing while `busybox foo` works, which reads as a broken kernel rather than a
# forgotten regeneration step.
#
# `--install -s` also gets the *target* right without being told: it readlinks
# bb_busybox_exec_path and falls back to argv[0] when that is not a link, so
# spawning it as `/bin/busybox` is the whole of the wiring.
#
# INSTALL_APPLET_SYMLINKS stays off because that is about `make install` writing
# links at *build* time, and these have to be written at *boot* time -- onto a
# filesystem that does not exist until the kernel has created it.
# CONFIG_BUSYBOX and CONFIG_FEATURE_INSTALLER, together, are what make
# `busybox --install -s /bin` exist.
#
# Both are needed and neither alone does anything: the whole `--install` block
# lives inside `#if ENABLE_BUSYBOX`, and FEATURE_INSTALLER `depends on` BUSYBOX,
# so enabling only the second is silently dropped by oldconfig. The result is that
# `--install` is not an option at all, falls through to "run the applet named
# --install", and exits 127 -- which is POSIX's "command not found" and reads as
# a spawn problem rather than a missing build option.
#
# BUSYBOX is worth having for the other half of what it provides: `busybox
# --list` is the authoritative answer to "which applets are in this build", which
# is what makes the applet list something the binary reports rather than something
# the kernel has to keep a copy of.
CONFIG_BUSYBOX=y
CONFIG_FEATURE_INSTALLER=y

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The shell.
#
# ash, because it is the smallest of busybox's shells that is a real shell. The
# line editor and the history file are part of ash itself rather than a separate
# library, which is the reason it is a megabyte of program and hush is not
# smaller by enough to matter.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
CONFIG_ASH=y
CONFIG_SH_IS_ASH=y
CONFIG_SHELL_ASH=y
CONFIG_BASH_IS_NONE=y

# Run an applet out of the shell's own builtin slot rather than forking it. This
# is most of what makes a busybox shell feel fast, and it costs one comparison
# per command.
CONFIG_FEATURE_SH_STANDALONE=y

# NOT enabled, and the reason is worth writing down because the option is
# described as costing nothing.
#
# NOFORK runs an applet by calling its main() inside the shell process: no fork,
# no exec, one comparison per command. It needs the applet to share the shell's
# stdio, allocator and globals, and it gets that by saving and restoring the
# ones it knows about.
#
# On this system `ls /` printed nothing at all. Not an error, no output, prompt
# straight back -- and `ls` run as an ordinary exec of `/bin/busybox` listed the
# directory correctly, from the same binary and the same libc. So the applet was
# fine and the in-process path was not.
#
# What it does that a fork does not: it never execs, so it never rebuilds the
# process image. The applet's own libc state -- stdio buffers already holding
# the shell's output, the allocator's arena, `errno` -- is whatever the shell
# left behind, and the save area covers the busybox globals but not mlibc's.
# An applet that writes through stdio therefore writes into buffers that were
# filled for output the shell has already "sent", and its output goes nowhere.
#
# Forking costs a page-table copy and costs it honestly, which is the trade this
# system wants: a command that prints what it was asked to print, with nothing
# shared with the shell that could swallow it.
# CONFIG_FEATURE_SH_NOFORK is not set

# Job control. Not speculative: the pty layer implements process groups, setsid
# and TIOCSPGRP, and without this the shell would be unable to use what it has.
CONFIG_ASH_JOB_CONTROL=y
CONFIG_CTTYHACK=y

# $((...)) arithmetic, in 64 bits. The shell is where arithmetic happens on a
# system with no bc and no compiler.
CONFIG_FEATURE_SH_MATH=y
CONFIG_FEATURE_SH_MATH_64=y
CONFIG_FEATURE_SH_MATH_BASE=y

# ash builtins. Each is a case where the shell answering directly is both smaller
# and faster than forking an applet: `echo` is a write, `test` is a branch, and
# `printf` is a format call. ASH_CMDCMD is the `command` keyword, which is how a
# script says "this is the program, not a builtin" and is also what ash's
# standalone-lookup needs in order to be able to find it.
CONFIG_ASH_ALIAS=y
CONFIG_ASH_ECHO=y
CONFIG_ASH_PRINTF=y
CONFIG_ASH_TEST=y
CONFIG_ASH_HELP=y
CONFIG_ASH_GETOPTS=y
CONFIG_ASH_CMDCMD=y

# $RANDOM. Two lines of state, and a script that seeds a name or picks a delay
# wants it.
CONFIG_ASH_RANDOM_SUPPORT=y

# Interactive prompt expansion. Without it the prompt cannot show the current
# directory, which is the one thing a prompt is for.
CONFIG_ASH_EXPAND_PRMT=y

# Keep history between shells. ash's own mechanism; the size limit is what stops
# an unbounded file on a filesystem with no quota.
CONFIG_FEATURE_SH_HISTFILESIZE=y

# Hide the "can't find command" chatter on an interactive shell, so that a
# mistyped command does not scroll away what the user was reading.
CONFIG_FEATURE_SH_EXTRA_QUIET=y

